# Books stack secrets (services VM)
# Copy to .sops.env and encrypt with:
#   SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE=../../sops_age_key.txt sops -e -i .sops.env
#
# Key-set contract: this file, the real .sops.env and the test fixture
# tests/fixtures/books.sops.env must all carry EXACTLY the same keys — the
# stack-env-drift lint fails on any difference.
#
# Reminder from CLAUDE.md: any value containing '$' must be single-quoted in
# the plaintext before encrypting — the decrypt-and-source path expands it
# otherwise. (None of the generate commands below emit '$'.)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Kavita JWT signing key, rendered into appsettings.json by kavita-config-init.
#
# This one is NOT optional, and its failure mode is silent: Kavita generates a
# key itself if the file lacks one, but every writer of appsettings.json is
# wrapped in a bare `catch { /* Swallow */ }`, so if that write ever fails the
# server carries on signing JWTs with the placeholder key that ships in the
# public repository — no crash, no log line. Pre-seeding is the fix.
#
# LENGTH IS LOAD-BEARING, and this is the second silent failure hiding here:
# Kavita signs with HMAC-SHA512 over the raw UTF-8 bytes of this string and
# refuses anything under 512 bits, so the key needs at least 64 characters.
# `openssl rand -hex 32` produces exactly 64 — right on the boundary — so use
# -hex 64 (128 characters) and leave no doubt. A short key does NOT fail at
# startup: the server comes up healthy, and then every register and login
# fails inside the handler and surfaces as an opaque 400.
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 64
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
KAVITA_TOKEN_KEY=changeme_openssl_rand_hex_64

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Kavita local admin, seeded headlessly by books-init (POST
# /api/Account/register works exactly once; a second run gets a 400, which the
# script treats as "already seeded"). Kavita's only password rule is length
# >= 6; usernames allow a-zA-Z0-9-._@+/
#
# These are ALSO what the shelfmark post-download hook logs in with to trigger
# a library scan — change them here and the hook follows automatically.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
KAVITA_ADMIN_USER=idan
KAVITA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=changeme_strong_password

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Audiobookshelf root user (POST /init, guarded by GET /status).
#
# Use the SAME email-or-username as your Authentik user if you want your OIDC
# login to BE the admin: ABS links OIDC identities by email
# (authOpenIDMatchExistingBy), so a match merges them on first login and a
# mismatch leaves you with two accounts. Merging afterwards is manual surgery.
#
# An EMPTY password here would create a passwordless root user with a 200
# response and only a warning in the log — books-init refuses to POST in that
# case, and the books suite asserts an empty-password login fails.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ABS_ADMIN_USER=idan
ABS_ADMIN_PASSWORD=changeme_strong_password

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Audiobookshelf's JWT signing secret. The app reads this EXACT variable name,
# so it is not renamed to an ABS_ prefix. Without it the secret is generated
# into the database, which means a /config restore-from-nothing invalidates
# every session and the secret cannot be rotated.
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
JWT_SECRET_KEY=changeme_openssl_rand_hex_32

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OIDC client secrets — LEAVE EMPTY until the Authentik side exists.
#
# Empty is a deliberate, complete state, not a placeholder (annex §8.4):
#   - Kavita's `Enabled` is a computed getter over (Authority, ClientId,
#     Secret), so an empty secret cleanly disables OIDC and local accounts
#     keep working.
#   - books-init skips the audiobookshelf auth-settings PATCH entirely.
# Neither app ends up advertising a login button that cannot work.
#
# To turn either on you need, for that service: the same secret added to
# headscale-vps/secrets.sops.yaml (+ .example + both test fixtures), a
# <svc>-oidc.yaml blueprint, the !Env var in the authentik worker's compose,
# a sops.placeholder line in modules/authentik.nix — then a VPS rebuild, then
# re-run this stack so the config is re-rendered/re-PATCHed. Redirect URIs and
# scopes are in the annex §3.1 (kavita) and §3.3 (audiobookshelf).
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
KAVITA_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
ABS_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Anna's Archive, for shelfmark's direct-download source.
#
# AA_MIRROR_URLS is REQUIRED for that source to work at all: shelfmark 1.3.x
# ships it empty and no mirror is baked into the image, so a deployment with
# only a donator key set has a working UI and nothing to search. Comma
# separated, no trailing slash.
#
# AA_DONATOR_KEY is optional — it is what buys the fast download endpoints
# instead of the browser wait/queue. Leave empty to use the slow path.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
AA_MIRROR_URLS=
AA_DONATOR_KEY=
